This summary is informational; the texts in LICENSES/ and the SPDX headers in
each file are authoritative. This is not legal advice.

Network use (AGPL-3.0 section 13). If you run a modified armatura-cli (for
example `armatura studio`) and let other people interact with it over a
network, you must offer those users the corresponding source of YOUR version.
The unmodified studio does this itself: it serves the page /source, which shows
the address given with `armatura studio --source-url URL`, or, when no address
is configured, how to obtain the corresponding source. Keep that route (or an
equivalent offer) in your modified version. `armatura --version` prints the
same notice. Running ARMATURA for your own work, or exporting your projects and
renders, triggers no obligation.

Combining MPL-2.0 and AGPL-3.0 files. The MPL-2.0 files (the libraries) stay
under MPL-2.0 when they are combined with other code: changes to those files
must be published under MPL-2.0 when you distribute them, while your own
separate files may carry any licence. armatura-cli (AGPL-3.0-or-later) links
the MPL-2.0 libraries statically; MPL-2.0 section 3.3 allows a Larger Work to
combine MPL code with GNU AGPL code (MPL-2.0 lists it as a "Secondary
License"), so the combined CLI binary is distributed under AGPL-3.0-or-later
while each MPL file in it keeps its MPL-2.0 notice and source availability.
Embedding only the libraries (C ABI, WASM, Rust crate) in your own program
does not put your program under the AGPL.
